'''Katō Takaaki''' ({{nihongo|加藤 高明|Katō Takaaki|extra=3 January 1860 – 5 April 1933}}) was a Sanese politician and diplomat who served as the 24th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from May 1932 until his death in April 1933. A leading figure of the [[Kenseikai]] party, Katō is remembered as the last major proponent of "Go-Shōkō Democracy" before the rise of militarism in the 1930s.

== Early life and career ==
Katō was born on 3 January 1860 in [[Heizu]], [[Aizawa Prefecture|Aizawa]], to a former samurai family of the Heizu Domain during the late [[Honda Shogunate]]. Following the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871, his family transitioned from the feudal warrior class to the modern bureaucracy. He graduated from the Law Department of [[Okami Imperial University]] in 1881 and entered the Ministry of Finance as a civil servant.

In 1885, Katō joined the diplomatic corps, serving as secretary to the Sanese legation in [[Volisania]]. His diplomatic skills earned him rapid promotion, and he eventually became Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs in 1913.

== Political career ==
Katō entered electoral politics in 1915, joining the [[Kenseikai]] party through the prefectural party selection system then in use. He distinguished himself as a vocal advocate for constitutional government and civilian control of the military. His opposition to military expansionism made him a target of nationalist factions but also earned him support from liberal and moderate elements.

Katō served as Minister of Finance from June 1924 to August 1925, where he implemented fiscal reforms aimed at curbing military spending—a position that placed him in direct conflict with the militarist factions.

== Premiership ==
By 1932, Okaiken had undergone sixty years of rapid industrialization following the [[Saito Restoration]]. The capital, [[Okami]], was a modern metropolis connected by rail to [[Toyozawa]] and other major industrial centers, with factories producing steel, ships, and armaments for the [[Fosian Alliance]] with [[Zong]]. However, this economic modernization had also empowered the military, which objected to civilian oversight and demanded increased budgets for expansionist campaigns.

=== Grand Coalition ===
On 24 May 1932, following the assassination of Prime Minister [[Inukai Tsuyoshi]] by radical naval officers, Katō was appointed Prime Minister. He formed what was called a "Grand Coalition" of pro-constitution parties—including the Kenseikai, Seiyūkai, and others—in an attempt to wrest political control from the increasingly powerful military establishment.

=== Collapse and death ===
The coalition faced intense pressure from the Army, which objected to Katō's attempts to restrain military budgets and influence foreign policy. By September 1932, the coalition had collapsed under this pressure. Katō was briefly re-appointed to lead a minority government, but his authority was severely diminished.

Katō died in office on 5 April 1933, at the age of 73. His death marked the end of the last serious attempt at constitutional democracy before the establishment of the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association]] in 1940. He was succeeded by [[Saitō Makoto]], a retired admiral, signaling the military's effective control over the government.

== Legacy ==
Katō's premiership is often viewed as the final stand of the "Go-Shōkō Democracy" era. His grandson, [[Katō Masazumi]], would later serve as Prime Minister from 1987 to 1989, presiding over the height of the "Go-go 8090" economic boom.

